Package: infiltratoros
Version: @VERSION@
Section: misc
Priority: optional
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: Infiltrator Projects <noreply@infiltrator-projects.invalid>
Depends: plymouth, apt, wget, ca-certificates, bash, fontconfig, python3, calendar (>= 1.0.125), libgtk-3-0 | libgtk-3-0t64
Description: InfiltratorOS branding and native application integration
 Installs the canonical InfiltratorOS Plymouth splash for Debian and
 Linux Mint derived systems, selects it as the active theme, rebuilds
 initramfs, and replaces distribution System Monitor and Calculator packages
 with Infiltrator builds compiled aggressively for the local CPU.
 .
 The independently published Infiltrator Calendar package is installed as a
 dependency. A backed-up per-user Cinnamon login migration swaps the stock
 calendar applet's panel entry for the Infiltrator applet without removing the
 Cinnamon package or altering any other panel items.
 .
 Files is installed as an additional native file manager in /Programs and is
 compiled with the same hardware-native policy. InfiltratorOS does not purge
 the existing distribution file manager or change the current default handler.
 .
 Defragmenter is added through its checksum-verified native compiler with
 CPU-native -O3 and LTO code generation. No other filesystem utilities are
 removed and its user-facing programs and private engines occupy the native /Programs and
 /System hierarchy.
 .
 The operating system downloads the latest source installer from each of the
 31 calendar and 23 clock repositories. Each module is built locally with
 aggressive CPU-native optimization, LTO and measured two-pass PGO, checked
 and installed as its own Debian package in the system resource folders.
 Removing a consumer does not remove these independent implementations.
 .
 Two independent FAT filesystem jobs install FAT12.so and FAT16.so using the
 respective checksum-verified native RUN packages. Each module is separately
 owned and verified under /System/Filesystems; neither job installs a kernel
 or UEFI driver.
 .
 Heavy native application builds are serialised so they do not compete
 for package locks or CPU.
